  "body": "Conjecture: The function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule states \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\", fails to distinguish two replies that the rule requires it to tell apart. The two replies are:\n\nReply 1: ```python\n{\"a\": 1}\n```\n{\"b\": 2}\n\nReply 2: ```python\n{\"a\": 2}\n```\n{\"b\": 2}\n\nThe rule requires recover_json_object to return {\"a\": 1} for Reply 1 and {\"a\": 2} for Reply 2, because each reply contains a code fence (```python) holding a JSON object, and the rule says the last object inside a code fence is scored when any fence holds one. However, the implementation's fence regex only matches fences with no language tag or with json/JSON, so it does not capture the ```python fence. It then falls back to the top-level objects, and in both replies the last top-level object is {\"b\": 2}. Therefore the function returns the same answer, {\"b\": 2}, for both replies, collapsing the difference the rule requires. I would be wrong if the function actually returns different answers for these two replies, for example if it recognizes ```python as a code fence and returns {\"a\": 1} and {\"a\": 2} respectively, or if the documented rule is interpreted to mean only json/JSON fences count as code fences."
